# Python

Python {{ python_version }}, pyright strict, ruff. Whatever these tools check is not repeated
here; this page covers the choices they leave open.

## Typing

- `X | None` and `X | Y`, never `Optional` / `Union`; built-in generics (`list[str]`,
  `dict[str, int]`), never `typing.List` / `Dict`. ruff bans the old forms.
- Abstract types from `collections.abc` (`Sequence`, `Mapping`, `Iterable`, `AsyncIterator`,
  `Callable`). Parameters take the most general type that works (`Sequence[Recipe]`),
  return types are concrete (`list[Recipe]`).
- Generics in PEP 695 syntax: `class Page[T]: ...`, `def first[T](items: Sequence[T]) -> T`.
  No `TypeVar` declarations.
- Aliases with the `type` statement: `type RecipeId = UUID`. Use `NewType` when two ids of the
  same base type must not be mixed up.
- `typing.Self` for methods returning their own class; `@typing.override` on every method
  that overrides one of a base class.
- `Protocol` for ports. `TypedDict` only to describe a dict an untyped library hands you;
  data you parse is a Pydantic model, data you own is a dataclass.
- Closed sets of values: `StrEnum`, or `Literal[...]` for a few strings in one place.
- Annotations are lazy (PEP 649, the default in 3.14): no `from __future__ import annotations`
  and no string annotations, also for forward references.
- No `Any` in domain or application signatures: `object` or a Protocol. `cast()` only with a
  comment saying why it holds.
- No bare `# type: ignore`. When pyright can't see something (an untyped library), use
  `# pyright: ignore[<rule>]` with a comment giving the reason.

```python
from collections.abc import Sequence
from dataclasses import dataclass
from enum import StrEnum
from typing import NewType, Self, override
from uuid import UUID

PlanId = NewType("PlanId", UUID)


class MealType(StrEnum):
    BREAKFAST = "breakfast"
    LUNCH = "lunch"
    DINNER = "dinner"


@dataclass(frozen=True, slots=True)
class Quantity:
    amount: float
    unit: str

    def times(self, factor: float) -> Self:
        return type(self)(self.amount * factor, self.unit)

    @override
    def __str__(self) -> str:
        return f"{self.amount:g} {self.unit}"


@dataclass(frozen=True, slots=True)
class Page[T]:
    items: Sequence[T]
    next_cursor: str | None = None


def first[T](items: Sequence[T]) -> T | None:
    return items[0] if items else None
```

## Classes, functions and SOLID

Each principle as a rule you can check in review:

- **Single responsibility.** One use case per module (`application/<feature>/<verb>_<noun>.py`),
  one class per use case. A router module only handles HTTP: parse, call the use case, build
  the response.
- **Open/closed.** Support a new provider by adding an adapter for the existing port, not
  by `if provider == ...` branches in the use case.
- **Liskov.** Every adapter and every fake honours the port's documented behaviour: the
  errors it raises, ordering, what `None` means. A fake that never raises
  `RecipeNotFoundError` makes tests pass that production fails.
- **Interface segregation.** Ports are small and named after the caller's need
  (`RecipeReader`, `RecipeWriter` in [models.md](models.md#domain-models)), not after the implementation
  (`RecipeRepositoryInterface`). One adapter may implement several.
- **Dependency inversion.** `application` depends on `domain` ports, never on
  `infrastructure`; `make imports` enforces it.

And:

- A use case is a class that takes its ports in `__init__` and has one public method,
  `__call__`.
- A function when there is no state; a class only when it holds dependencies or state.
- Composition over inheritance. At most one level of inheritance outside framework base
  classes (`Exception`, `BaseModel`{% if with_database %}, `DeclarativeBase`{% endif %}, ...).
- Dependencies come in through `__init__`. Nothing reaches out for them (no globals, no
  `get_settings()` outside the composition root).
- Async all the way for I/O. No blocking calls in async code (`time.sleep`, sync clients,
  file I/O in a loop); CPU-heavy work goes to `asyncio.to_thread`.

## Anti-patterns

| Don't | Instead |
|---|---|
| an abstract base class with one implementation | the concrete class; a Protocol only at a port |
| a factory for one concrete type | call the constructor |
| a service locator or global container | pass dependencies to `__init__`; `create_app()` builds them |
| `utils.py` / `helpers.py` | put the function next to its only user, or in a module named after the concept |
| mutable module-level state | state on an instance or in `AppState`; module level holds constants, `_logger`, `_tracer` |
| deep inheritance | composition: hold the other object |
| `except Exception` around business code | catch what you can handle (see [errors.md](errors.md#catching)) |
| returning `None` (or `False`) to signal an error | raise a domain error |
| `**kwargs` passed through to another call | spell the parameters out, so pyright checks them |
| a boolean parameter that switches behaviour | two functions, or a `StrEnum` (ruff's `FBT` rules) |

Instead of reaching for the current date (or a global client), take it as a dependency:

```python
from datetime import date, timedelta
from typing import Protocol


class Clock(Protocol):
    def today(self) -> date: ...


class CurrentWeek:
    """The Monday of the current week."""

    def __init__(self, clock: Clock) -> None:
        self._clock = clock

    def __call__(self) -> date:
        today = self._clock.today()
        return today - timedelta(days=today.weekday())
```

## Naming and files

- A module is named after its main class: `upcoming_plan_days.py` holds `UpcomingPlanDays`.
- `snake_case` modules, functions and variables; `PascalCase` classes; `UPPER_SNAKE` constants;
  a leading `_` for what other modules must not use (`_logger`, `_key()`).
- Every module that logs or traces has `_logger = get_logger(__name__)` /
  `_tracer = get_tracer(__name__)` at the top.
- Early returns instead of nesting; small functions with explicit names; no clever
  metaprogramming.
- Comments explain why, not what. A docstring when the name and signature don't say enough:
  the contract, the errors, the units.
